<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day14" xml:id="day-14-lecture">
    <title>Day 14</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 5.1.4-5: More cross-validation
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <objectives>
        <title>Covered in this class</title>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Bias-variance tradeoff
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Bias-Variance Tradeoff: Bias</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                E(y_0 - \hat{f}(x_0))^2
    = \operatorname{Var}(\hat{f}(x_0))
    + [\operatorname{Bias}(\hat{f}(x_0))]^2
    + \operatorname{Var}(\epsilon)
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            We are discussing bias in the estimation of test error rate.
        </p>

        <sbsgroup>
            <sidebyside>
                <p>
                    Validation set: High bias.
                    It overestimates test error because it uses a small subset
                    of the data.
                </p>

                <image source="figures/validation_set_pessimistic_bias.png" width="80%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Learning curve monotonically decreasing and a point with
                        training sample less than the total number of sample
                        points
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>

            <sidebyside>
                <p>
                    LOOCV: Gives an approximately unbiased estimate because it
                    uses almost all the data each time ( <m>n-1</m>
                    observations) for training.
                </p>

                <image source="figures/LOOCV_low_bias.png" width="80%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Learning curve monotonically decreasing and a point with
                        training sample equals to almost the total number of
                        samples.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>

            <sidebyside>
                <p>
                    <m>k</m>-fold CV: has a medium level of bias because each
                    training set has approximately <m>(k-1)n/k</m> observations.
                </p>

                <image source="figures/kfold_med_bias.png" width="80%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Learning curve monotonically decreasing and a point with
                        training sample equals to a large portion of the total
                        number of samples.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </sidebyside>
        </sbsgroup>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Bias-Variance Tradeoff: Variance</title>

        <p>
            <md>
                E(y_0 - \hat{f}(x_0))^2
    = \operatorname{Var}(\hat{f}(x_0))
    + [\operatorname{Bias}(\hat{f}(x_0))]^2
    + \operatorname{Var}(\epsilon)
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            We are discussing variance in the estimation of test error rate.
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="50%">
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Validation set has high variance:
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The estimate of test error is highly
                                        dependent on the particular split of the
                                        data.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            LOOCV has high variance:
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        It averages the outputs of <m>n</m>
                                        fitted models, each trained on almost
                                        identical observations.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The fitted models are highly correlated
                                        with each other.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The mean of highly correlated quantities
                                        has higher variance than the mean of
                                        uncorrelated quantities.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <!-- <li>
                                    <p>
                                        This is like a repeated-measures problem
                                        in science; the results may not
                                        generalize well across samples.
                                    </p>
                                </li> -->
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>k</m>-fold CV: Medium variance.
                            <ul>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The <m>k</m> fitted models are somewhat
                                        less correlated with each other.
                                    </p>
                                </li>

                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        Empirically, <m>k=5</m> or <m>10</m> has
                                        been shown to be a happy medium.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ul>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>In short: Validation vs LOOCV vs <m>k</m>-Fold CV</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We often treat the validation set as if it were the test
                        set, even though it is not.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        A true test set is an independent sample.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Cross-validation measures never provide that independent
                        sample.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <tabular>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="40%"/>
            <col width="40%"/>
            <row header="yes">
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        Method
                    </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        Bias
                    </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        Variance
                    </p>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        Validation Set Approach
                    </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        High: Consistently overestimates the true test error
                        because of small training size.
                    </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        High: Very sensitive to the choice of the splits.
                    </p>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        <m>k</m>-Fold Cross-Validation
                    </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        Medium: Uses a large proportion of the data (
                        <m>(k-1)/k\,N</m> ) for training.
                    </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        Medium: Averaging across the folds reduces the effect of
                        the randomly obtained folds.
                    </p>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        Leave-One-Out (LOOCV)
                    </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        Low: Uses almost all ( <m>N-1</m> ) data points for
                        training.
                    </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                    <p>
                        High: The <m>N</m> models are trained on nearly
                        identical data, so the resulting models are highly
                        correlated.
                    </p>
                </cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </paragraphs>

<exercise>
<task>

<statement>
    <p>If we use 10-fold cross-validation, what percentage of the data is used for training?</p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            The model is trained on <m>(k-1)/k</m> of the data, where <m>k</m> is the number of folds.
        </p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
        <p>
            with <m>k=10</m> we have <m>\frac{k-1}{k} = \frac{9}{10} = 0.9</m>, so 90% of the data is used for training.
        </p>
    </answer>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we use 5-fold cross-validation, what percentage of the data is used for training?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                The model is trained on <m>(k-1)/k</m> of the data, where <m>k</m> is the number of folds.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
            <p>
                with <m>k=5</m> we have <m>\frac{k-1}{k} = \frac{4}{5} = 0.8</m>, so 80% of the data is used for training.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we use leave-one-out cross-validation, what percentage of the data is used for training?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                In LOOCV, each training set has <m>n-1</m> observations out of <m>n</m> total observations.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
            <p>
                with <m>k=n</m> we have <m>\frac{k-1}{k} = \frac{n-1}{n}</m>, so approximately 90% of the data is used for training when <m>n</m> is large.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                For any two different folds in <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation, how many training sets are shared between them?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Each training set in <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation has <m>(k-1)/k</m> of the data.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
            <p>
                For any two different folds in <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation, <m>(k-2)/(k-1)</m> of the training sets are shared between them.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                For any two different folds in <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation, what is the number of overlapping samples?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                For any two different folds in <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation, <m>(k-2)/(k-1)</m> of the training sets are shared between them.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <hint>
            <p>
                The number of overlapping samples is <m>(k-2)/(k-1)</m> times the number of samples in a training set.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
            <p>
                The number of overlapping samples is <m>(k-2)/(k-1)N</m>, where <m>N</m> is the number of samples in the training set.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the the percentage overlap between any two different training sets in <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation?
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Each training set in <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation has <m>(k-1)/k</m> of the data.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
            <p>
                The percentage overlap between any two different training sets in <m>k</m>-fold cross-validation is <m>\frac{k-2}{k-1}</m>.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </task>
</exercise>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Using K-Fold CV on Polynomial Linear Regression</title>

        <p>
            Polynomial regression
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="45%">
            <p>
                Replace the linear model
                <md>
                    y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + \epsilon_i
                </md>
                with
                <md>
                    y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + \beta_2 x_i^2
          + \cdots + \beta_d x_i^d + \epsilon_i
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            These models can be fit with linear regression by
                            passing in predictors <m>x_i^\ell</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We tend not to go higher than degree 3 or 4 because
                            the model can become overly flexible.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>
